 # Opacity-based Early Termination
-
+Also called: Early Ray Termination (ERT), Opacity Thresholding, Alpha Cutoff
 
 ## Overview
-Opacity-based Early Termination is a family of acceleration techniques for image-order volume rendering that reduces computational cost by halting ray traversal when the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby neglecting contributions from distant or occluded regions [S1][S11, unofficial][S1]. It solves the problem of redundant sampling in transparent or semi-transparent volumes by exploiting the physical property that once a ray strikes an opaque object or traverses sufficient density, the accumulated color stabilizes and further samples do not significantly alter the final pixel color [S1][S8, unofficial][S9, unofficial]. Unlike siblings such as [Russian Roulette](1371-russian-roulette.md) which use probabilistic survival to maintain unbiased estimators, standard opacity-based methods typically use deterministic thresholds, which can introduce systematic bias [S16, unofficial][S22, unofficial].
+Opacity-based Early Termination is a family of volume rendering optimizations that halt the front-to-back ray integration loop once the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby skipping samples that contribute negligibly to the final pixel color. It differs from [Empty-space skipping](1002-empty-space-skipping.md) by operating on the accumulated transmittance of the ray path rather than spatially culling empty voxels, and from [Russian Roulette](1371-russian-roulette.md) by using a deterministic cutoff rather than a probabilistic one, which introduces a systematic bias in exchange for reduced noise and computational cost.
 
